# Do not download onnxruntime-node's CUDA execution-provider binaries.
#
# `onnxruntime-node` arrives transitively via @huggingface/transformers, which
# powers the CLIP *text* encoder in lib/clip.ts. That runs on CPU inside a
# Vercel serverless function; there is no GPU anywhere in this system, and
# nothing in the codebase names a CUDA execution provider. The ~300 MB GPU
# tarball was pure download weight even when it worked.
#
# It stopped working on 2026-11-17: the postinstall script fetches
# https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/releases/download/v1.21.0/onnxruntime-linux-x64-gpu-1.21.0.tgz
# which now 404s, so `npm ci` fails outright — in CI, on Vercel, and on any
# fresh clone. The asset is gone upstream, so this is permanent rather than a
# flake to retry. Skipping it is onnxruntime's own documented remedy, quoted in
# the error it raises: "You will still be able to use ONNX Runtime, but the
# CUDA EP will not be available."
#
# npm turns this key into npm_config_onnxruntime_node_install_cuda for
# lifecycle scripts, which is one of the two inputs
# node_modules/onnxruntime-node/script/install.js reads (the other being the
# ONNXRUNTIME_NODE_INSTALL_CUDA env var). Both were verified against the real
# script by moving the installed CUDA .so aside and re-running it: without a
# flag it reproduces the 404 and exits 1; with either flag it exits 0.
#
# npm prints "Unknown project config" for this key and warns it will stop
# working in the next major version — unavoidable, since it belongs to a
# third-party postinstall rather than to npm. It is kept anyway because it is
# the only form that applies automatically to a fresh `npm ci` and to Vercel's
# install step. CI additionally sets the env-var form, which is not
# deprecated; see .github/workflows/ci.yml.
onnxruntime-node-install-cuda=skip
